  "description": "A parametric WALL WASHER on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT — the wall being lit. The fittings stand off it by the offset, exactly as a cove straddles its depth.\n\nWASH OR GRAZE IS ONE NUMBER, NOT A MODE: how far off the wall it stands. A third of the wall height washes it evenly; 100 mm grazes it and shows every trowel mark in the plaster. The select is carried so the intent survives into a schedule, but `recommendedOffset` is the thing to read.\n\nSPACING OVER OFFSET IS THE EVENNESS RULE. Near 1 the scallops merge into a wash; much above it and you get a row of separate pools with dark between them. It is the one ratio that decides whether a wall washer works, and it costs nothing to report.\n\nTHE WASH BAND IS TRIGONOMETRY ON THE AIM. A ray at angle a from straight down covers the offset after dropping `offset / tan(a)`, so the beam edges give the top and bottom of the lit band and the wall height clips it. Steeper aim, lower hit, shorter wash.\n\nAVERAGE WALL ILLUMINANCE IS LUMENS OVER THE LIT AREA — a sanity figure for a schedule, not a simulation.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: four 650 lm fittings on a 3 m wall, 900 mm off it — a third of its height — squeezed from 800 to 750 mm apart because four will not fit at 800: 2600 lm over a band from the floor to 2.07 m, about 419 lx, spacing over offset 0.83.\n\nWHAT THIS CANNOT DO. The wall is flat and straight. No scallop shape, no true asymmetric distribution — that lives in the lamp file.",
